🔥 𝗞𝘂𝗯𝗲𝗿𝗻𝗲𝘁𝗲𝘀 𝗜𝗻𝗴𝗿𝗲𝘀𝘀 𝗶𝘀 𝗡𝗼𝘁 𝗪𝗼𝗿𝗸𝗶𝗻𝗴 — 𝗛𝗼𝘄 𝗪𝗶𝗹𝗹 𝗬𝗼𝘂 𝗧𝗿𝗼𝘂𝗯𝗹𝗲𝘀𝗵𝗼𝗼𝘁 𝗜𝘁?

𝗜𝗻𝘁𝗲𝗿𝘃𝗶𝗲𝘄 𝗤𝘂𝗲𝘀𝘁𝗶𝗼𝗻:

👉 “Your Kubernetes application works internally, but users cannot access it through the Ingress URL. How will you troubleshoot it?”

In production, I wouldn't immediately restart the Ingress Controller. I'd trace the request path step by step.

🔍 𝗠𝘆 𝗧𝗿𝗼𝘂𝗯𝗹𝗲𝘀𝗵𝗼𝗼𝘁𝗶𝗻𝗴 𝗔𝗽𝗽𝗿𝗼𝗮𝗰𝗵

1️⃣ Check the Ingress resource

kubectl get ingress -n <namespace>
kubectl describe ingress <ingress-name> -n <namespace>

Verify the hostname, rules, paths and backend Service.

2️⃣ Check the Ingress Controller

kubectl get pods -A
kubectl logs <controller-pod> -n <controller-namespace>

Look for configuration errors and controller issues.

3️⃣ Verify the Service and endpoints

kubectl get svc -n <namespace>
kubectl get endpoints <service-name> -n <namespace>

Confirm that the Service has healthy, expected endpoints.

4️⃣ Check DNS resolution

nslookup <your-domain>

Verify that the domain resolves to the expected IP address.

5️⃣ Test the application internally

Test the Service from inside the cluster to determine whether the issue is with the application or external access.

6️⃣ Check external connectivity

Investigate load balancer health, firewall rules, network policies, ports 80/443 and TLS certificate configuration.

🎯 𝗧𝗵𝗲 𝗥𝗲𝗾𝘂𝗲𝘀𝘁 𝗙𝗹𝗼𝘄

User → DNS → Load Balancer → Ingress Controller → Service → Pod

Check each layer until you identify where the request fails.

💡 𝗞𝗲𝘆 𝗟𝗲𝗮𝗿𝗻𝗶𝗻𝗴

An application working inside Kubernetes does not guarantee that it is accessible externally.

Follow the request path, identify the failing layer, and fix the root cause.
